ology  in this area in terms of brain recording  techniques I mentioned deep brain  stimulation where wires are placed into  the brain currents injected in patients  with Parkinson's disease  these wires are now being developed to  have recording capabilities as well as  stimulating capabilities this will  enable continuous data to be obtained  from people who wanted to go this  therapeutic procedure so we'll have  data on brain activity in this region  where the wire is and that can be  correlated with behavior and correlated  with the current injection the  intervention that's made there are also  great advances that have been made in  the brain computer computer interface  world where very tiny chips have been  introduced in the brain  enabling recording from one or a few  neurons per per projection but these  chips can have hundreds of projections  enabling recording from hundreds of  thousands of neurons the power of this  has certainly been seen in cases in  which this brain electrical activity has  been used to control computers or  prosthetic devices what we've seen here  is that the neural activity coming out  of the brain is a mirror on how the  brain learns because when first put in  the neural activity cannot control the  device or the computer but the brain  learns how to do that over time so by  collecting this information we see brain  plasticity in real time so these are  just a couple of examples that are new  that we could add on to the MRI scanner  the high field MOR functional magnetic  resonance imaging magneto  encephalography intracranial Econ  recording of neural activity so yes  there are there have been many advances  and that's why we believe the time is  right to because these are stepping  stones to something even more miraculous  so thanks for that question  reports our DBS is working for  Huntington's question mark have you been  testing that more if it's helpful well I  think I was actually a neurologist who  took care of many Huntington's patients  over the years and I have not been aware  of DBS as a very effective treatment for  the disability that occurs in  Huntington's disease Huntington's  disease unfortunately affects large  areas of the brain multiple different  networks and connections there is a mode  of control disorder that's similar to  Parkinson's with slowness in  coordination this Korea which is an  involuntary movement but the biggest  problem is actually motor planning so  patients with Huntington's disease have  much have a great difficulty learning  motor control programs to do play the  piano wash the dishes and they they lose  these motor programs over time so the  the problem in Huntington's is very  complicated throughout multiple circuits  and would be very difficult to turn that  around by stimulating one brain area I  think where deep brain stimulation has  shown a lot of promise but still in the  investigative stage is in depression so  patients with depression that are  resistant to medications have been  helped by turning off of particular  brain region with deep brain stimulation  and so these disorders where there's a  central control region that is spreading  out into large areas of brain such as  the dopamine cells in the [ __ ] which  then release widely into the stratum  there's an example where stimulation  looks promising and depression similarly  there may be certain control centers  we're changing the activity in that one  center can have a massive effect but in  things like Alzheimer's in Huntington  disease the disorder is really quite  distributed throughout the brain thanks  very much for that question though which  are the companies that are helping in  tool development  another good question I hope they all  will it's actually something we're  actively working on is engaging industry  in the brain initiative so we've had  discussions with companies as far afield  as brain imaging companies to the  companies that develop intracranial  recording and stimulation technologies  to the companies that are involved in  managing and analyzing the huge streams  of data that come out of the brain when  one measures brain activity and  thousands of neurons simultaneously so  we think that there is a great interest  on the part of private industry to  become involved in the brain initiative  but I think it's a work in progress  aligning the different agendas in the  private industry groups in the public  groups and for certain areas of the  brain initiative they're more relevant  to industry than others I think we've  had the greatest pickup in the area of  brain stimulation and brain recording  intracranial brain stimulation recording  in humans so thanks for that question  well brain initiatives the interested in  linking brain function disorder to  environmental exposure well I think  looking into the brain and understanding  brain circuits is the current frontier  the next frontier I think is what this  question is getting at and that is  understanding environmental influences  on brain function and that has been very  difficult as mentioned our tools for  interrogating and monitoring brain  circuits are quite poor our tools for  monitoring environmental exposure over  time and humans are worse so I think  that's another area of discovery and  tool development that's crucially needed  so for instance a disease like  Parkinson's disease there is a genetic  component but it accounts for a minority  of the percentage of causality in  Parkinson's disease so that means to us  that there is an environmental component  but how to get it back  certainly there are very tantalizing  natural experiments that happened the  most prominent was the use of a drug  called mptp by drug abusers in  California they were trying to make a  recreational drug and instead made a  chemical that destroyed the neurons in  the substantia [ __ ] and caused severe  parkinsonism a dramatic example of how  environmental exposure leads to brain  diseases in Parkinson's disease the  question has been for a long time are  there other  less potent but longer exposures to  toxins that lead to the disease there  are some clues that pesticides may be in  that category but we have a real problem  in interrogating the an integral of the  exposure to toxins to understand how  they work to disturb neural circuits so  I think coming together in the end  will be our understanding of genomics  our understanding of brain circuit  function and then bringing in the  environmental exposures to see how they  interact in this triad that determines  who we are and how our brains function  so thanks again that was a great  question unfortunately we are out of  time I want to thank the audience for  their interesting questions and  participation in today's event we also  like to thank dr. corsets for his  informative presentation today's webcast  will be available for on-demand viewing  for several months following you'll  receive an email from lab roots alerting  you when this webcast will be available  for replay  I mean why you'd afford that  announcements your colleagues who may  have missed today's live event see you  next time goodbye
 

